From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:51:48 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Sun shadows: Wind Waker HD's normals and colours for the
 objects and the rooms, and its light on the scenery

Sun shadows: Wind Waker HD's normals and colours for the objects, and its light on the scenery

aurora_set_hd_object_tables(normals, colours): the HD pack's optional/hd-normals/objects.bin and
optional/hd-colours/objects.bin (from the player's HD dump), by the GameCube's raw joint-local positions as the
stage tables are. The normals are looked up after the stage's for the draws that take smooth normals; the
colours light the draws of the palms' kind as HD lights them. DOL_AURORA_HD_VERTEX_COLOURS still overrides the
colours for tests.

DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_HD_TERRAIN_LIGHT=1: every draw of the palms' kind that takes smooth normals
takes HD's light, the scenery's too: its shade colour black, its own vertex colour kept where no table has HD's.
HD's terrain material is the palms' rule, albedo x vertex colour x mix(ambient, sun, N.L x shadow), and its
vertex colours are the GameCube's baked greys (a Cemu probe): the GameCube's mix never goes below the shade
colour, so its greys under 1 stood lighter than HD's. Outset's bridge at 10:00, median brightness of the left
cliff: HD 64, before 76, after 62.

aurora_set_hd_stage_colours: the stage's rooms' own HD vertex colours, which the
draws with HD's light take before the objects' table, in the game's gamma (HD
multiplies its colours raw in linear light, the TEV in the game's gamma). With
them a dungeon room's floors and walls come within about 10% of HD's (Ganon's
Tower's entrance from 0.39 to 0.94 of HD's brightness).

HD's terrain VS takes its hemisphere (0.5 + 0.5 N.y between the ambient and the sky's light) per vertex from its own unit normals, blended linearly across each triangle. The sun pass took N.y from the depth's face (about 1 on the ground: the sky's colour alone, bluer than HD's where HD's ground normals lean, Windfall's spawn 7 path and the arch path). With DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_SMOOTH_AMBIENT=1 all scenery with a smooth normal now takes it; the mask pass normalizes each vertex's normal and writes the blend as it is, and the hemisphere takes its y without renormalizing (HD's per-vertex value). Palms keep theirs. =skinned keeps the old rule for comparison.

The smooth normals and HD's colours kept from frame to frame. with_smooth_normals() made them again for every lit
scenery draw every frame: a weld map over the draw's positions, its faces' normals, and a lookup per vertex in HD's
normals and colours. Hyrule Castle's hall is 66 such draws and 47,000 vertices a game frame, the same every frame, and
on an i5-6500 that was 7 ms of the GX worker's 20 and the 6% late frames there at 30 FPS.

Now a draw that comes again with the same bytes and the same inputs (the stage's and the objects' tables, and for HD's
light its shade and light colours) takes the vertices made the first time. The hash only picks the entry; the draw is
compared in full, so the vertices are the very ones the old path makes. Lava draws (their glow follows the game frame)
always take the old path. Entries not used for 120 game frames go, and past 1024 entries a new draw isn't kept.

- The cache is smooth_normal_cache.hpp, with tests (smooth_normal_cache_tests): a hit is the uncached bytes, any
  changed float, index or input misses, unused entries go, a full cache makes new draws uncached.
- DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_NORMAL_CACHE=0 makes them every frame, as before. _CACHE_LOG=1 prints the draws, vertices and
  hits a game frame.
- Debian i5-6500 + GTX 1660 SUPER, Hyrule Castle layer 2: 22.2 to 15.0 ms CPU per game frame unpaced; frames over 34
  ms at 30 FPS 5.8% to 0.1%, and the same turning the camera or walking (100% hits after the arrival). The frozen hall
  is the emulation thread's: -0.35 ms still, -1.3 turning. The pictures are the same.
